/**
 * components/muscleMapPaths.generated.ts
 *
 * GENERATED from assets/mm.svg — do not hand-edit.
 *
 * Source is a clean vector "muscle chart" illustration (front + back figure
 * side by side, single viewBox spanning 0-1248 x 0-832): every one of the
 * app's 14 tracked muscles gets its own visually distinct region already,
 * so classification here was mostly straightforward — matched by anatomical
 * position (front/back, vertical band, left/right symmetry), cross-checked
 * against a rendered, labeled preview of every shape before this file was
 * written. A handful of small medial-thigh/groin wedges (adductor-ish, no
 * matching entry in the Muscle enum) were deliberately left OUT of both
 * PATHS tables rather than force-mapped to the nearest unrelated muscle.
 *
 * PATH DATA IS SIMPLIFIED, not the raw trace — the source SVG was
 * auto-vectorized from a raster image, and every shape's bezier curves
 * carry small control-point noise that's invisible at a thin hairline
 * stroke but reads as messy/scribbly bumps once the outline is stroked
 * bold (reported: "the edges are just kinda messed up... overlap with a
 * bold brush"). Fixed at the data level, not by tuning stroke width down
 * to hide it: every subpath is flattened to a dense point polygon (bezier
 * sampling), simplified with Ramer-Douglas-Peucker (epsilon 1.6 in this
 * file's ~1248-wide coordinate space — strips sub-visual wobble, keeps
 * every real corner/curve of the shape), and re-emitted as straight line
 * segments between the surviving points. At render scale this reads as
 * clean confident edges, not faceted — confirmed by a zoomed render of the
 * abs six-pack (the densest, most adjacent-fragment region) before/after.
 *
 * 'silhouette' is the single complete full-body outline shape extracted from
 * the source (one shape per side, covering head/torso/arms/hands/legs/feet)
 * — rendered neutral, first/behind everything else. The source's other
 * background/shading sub-shapes (a redundant white body-cutout duplicate,
 * small highlight/shadow accent slivers) were deliberately NOT included:
 * verified by render that folding them in alongside the silhouette causes
 * SVG nonzero-fill-rule cancellation (near-duplicate overlapping shapes
 * wound in opposite directions cancel out, leaving rim slivers instead of a
 * solid body) — one clean shape per side renders correctly and is all this
 * app's flat-fill style needs.
 *
 * A few muscles are visible on BOTH the front and back figure (Traps,
 * Forearms, Calves) but this app tracks a rank per muscle from a single
 * canonical side (see MUSCLE_MAP_REGIONS below) — the other side's fragments
 * still render (in *_PATHS, under their muscle-slug id), just always neutral
 * gray, same convention as the previous source.
 *
 * MUSCLE_MAP_LABEL_POS — one rank-label anchor point per muscle, see its own
 * comment below for how it's picked.
 */

